issue.body:
Updates the no-else-return rule to display multiple errors in cases of nested violations of this rule, where before it was only showing the most interior violation and would not display the others until that one was fixed.

@nzakas Please review, thanks!  I'm not convinced this is the best implementation, but it seems to satisfy all the use-cases I could think of.

Fixes #1369

body:
I'm working on fixing this using proper tree traversal, but I'm running into a few edge case issues that are concerning.  You can find my branch here:

https://github.com/elwayman02/eslint-es6/tree/fix-else-return-traversal

Most cases work just fine, except when there are multiple nested if blocks where only some of them violate the rule.  I believe the issue resolves around my code's inability to successfully determine whether all code paths of the inner consequent block have return statements...especially when the consequent contains an if-statement that has no alternate.  Essentially, there are a few points where my code makes naive choices that cause these edge cases to fail.

Thoughts, @nzakas (or anyone)?  What can I do to surmount this problem?

Examples:

Should have 1 error, throwing 3

```
function foo() { 
    if (true) { 
        if (false) { 
            if (true) return x; 
            else w = y; 
        } else { 
            w = x; 
        } 
    } else { 
        return z; 
    } 
}
```

Should have 2 errors, throwing 1

```
function foo() { 
    if (true) { 
        if (false) { 
            if (true) return x; 
            else return y; 
        } 
        return w; 
    } else { 
        return z; 
    } 
}
```

Should have 2 errors, throwing 3

```
function foo() { 
    if (true) { 
        if (false) { 
            if (true) return x; 
            else return y; 
        } else { 
            w = x; 
        } 
    } else { 
        return z; 
    } 
}
```

What about this case?

```
if (val) {
    // do stuff
    if (val2) {
        return stuff;
    } else {
        doStuff();
        return otherStuff;
    }
} else {
    return thirdStuff;
}
```

Ok, I took your advice and stopped going down the rabbit hole.  I was able to write the code such that it doesn't try for infinite nesting but does catch several of the base nested cases (+1 block depth, essentially).

Additionally, any more complex examples like the one below should now report multiple errors, but not as many as there actually are (for example, 2 errors instead of 3):

```
function foo() { 
    if (true) { 
        if (false) { 
            if (true) return x; 
            else return y; 
        } else { 
            return w; 
        } 
    } else { 
        return z; 
    } 
}
```
